1) - edit the parameter file (example dummyfunction.input)
     and the Mathematica template (example dummyfunction.m)
   - in the parameter file specify the names of implicit functions
     via the input eg. 'dummys=dum1,dum2'
   - NEW since Jan 2015: you can also have epsilon-dependent dummy functions:
     in the parameter file specify the list "dummyorder", which should indicate 
     the highest power of eps contained in each dummy function. 
     The expansion is then done to the overall eps^epsord as usual.
   - in the template file, include any implicit functions with their
     arguments in the factorlist
   - in the output directory (you will have to create this manually) put
     files with the names of those functions you specified in the parameter
     file. These should contain a list of the arguments of the function,
     and the definition of the function in Mathematica syntax, via eg.
     intvars={a,b,c,d};
     dum1=a^2 + b^2 +c^2 +d^2 + 4 a b c d +2;
     cf dummyfunction/dum1.m
     
2) - from the 'general' directory, use the command
     './createdummyfortran.pl -d workingdir -p paramfile'
     this will write the optimized fortran code for the functions specified
     in the output directory.
   - if you wish to define a fortran function manually, you still need to 
     have a file in the output directory with the correct syntax so that the
     fortran header will be written for you, but you can then edit the fortran
     code yourself (eg testdummy/cut.m and testdummy/cut_edited.f)
     
3) - the program can now be used exactly as before.
   - if you wish to change the definition of an implicit function, just edit 
     the function and rerun createdummyfortran.pl. The algebraic part does not
     need to be redone. (NB that rerunning createdummyfortran.pl will overwrite
     any fortran functions edited manually, so a copy of these should be made if
     they are to be reused)

NB any function left implicit must be integrable within the integration domain
